{"id":"spcx-earnings-q3-2026","title":"SPCX earnings — SpaceX, Q3 2026","section":"Earnings","date":"2026-10-20","html":"<p>Management sees a shortage of AI compute that it can sell into as fast as it can build, and the thing that could spoil it is that the building is running far ahead of the cash the business makes.</p>\n<p>Reported 2026-10-20. Next report: not announced (one calendar site forecasts Feb 9, 2027). Updated 2026-11-12. Research, not investment advice.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-short-version\">The short version</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Demand:</strong> Stronger. Revenue $14.58B; the AI segment is now the largest at $7.56B; backlog $63B.</li><li><strong>Guidance:</strong> Raised on spending. Q4 capex $35–40B, up from $19.6B spent in Q3. First Q1 2027 revenue range: $33–37B. The $100B annualised revenue goal for year-end was held.</li><li><strong>Biggest worry:</strong> Spending far ahead of cash. $48.1B of capex in nine months, and debt up to $38.0B from $22.0B.</li><li><strong>Chart:</strong> Leans down, mildly. Price is under the 20- and 50-day. A daily close above $149.13 flips it up; a daily close under the $135 IPO price opens $120.39.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"the-numbers\">The numbers</h3>\n<ul><li>Revenue $14.58B against roughly $12.6–13.0B expected. GAAP EPS $0.35 against $0.10–0.16. A beat.</li><li>Net income of $4.4B included a $561M unrealised investment gain. Over nine months the company still shows a net loss of $413M.</li><li>By segment: AI $7.56B, Starlink $5.25B, launch $1.77B.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"what-management-said-about-the-road-ahead\">What management said about the road ahead</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Next quarter:</strong> Capex of $35–40B in Q4.</li><li><strong>Q1 2027:</strong> Revenue of $33–37B, the first range given. That is more than double Q3's $14.58B.</li><li><strong>Capacity:</strong> 1.7 GW of compute at quarter-end, 2.5–3 GW expected by year-end and about 10 GW more in 2027.</li><li><strong>Pricing:</strong> Running above the top of the range discussed last quarter. Management expects a shortage of AI compute.</li><li><strong>Payback:</strong> &quot;new compute deployments are generating a payback of less than a year.&quot; (Bret Johnsen, CFO)</li><li><strong>Starlink:</strong> 13.6 million consumer subscribers, up from 7.2 million a year ago.</li><li><strong>Backlog:</strong> $63B, about two-thirds due within a year.</li><li><strong>Aspiration, not guidance:</strong> Elon Musk spoke of $500B to $1 trillion of annualised revenue by the end of 2027. Treat that as a hope, not a forecast.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"what-management-is-worried-about\">What management is worried about</h3>\n<ol><li><strong>Power equipment and chips.</strong> The bottlenecks are power gear, especially turbine castings, and chip supply. They gate the 10 GW planned for 2027.</li><li><strong>Customer concentration.</strong> One AI customer was 34.5% of revenue and another 16.6%. Neither is named.</li><li><strong>The Tesla question.</strong> SpaceX and Tesla have each formed a special committee on a possible combination; no terms are agreed.</li><li><strong>Launch licensing.</strong> Named on the call; no figure given.</li><li><strong>Lawsuits and fines.</strong> The filing lists suits over AI image generation, an EU fine under appeal, and a suit over data-center turbine emissions. Amounts: not found.</li></ol>\n<p>The 10-Q states no material changes to risk factors since the prospectus.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-big-worry-spending-far-ahead-of-cash-both-ways\">The big worry: spending far ahead of cash, both ways</h3>\n<p><strong>What would ease it</strong></p>\n<ul><li>The payback claim holds. If new compute really pays back in under a year, the spending funds itself quickly.</li><li>Revenue lands in the $33–37B range for Q1 2027. That is the first hard test of the buildout.</li><li>The cash pile. About $89.9B of cash and securities at quarter-end.</li><li>Cheaper launch. Starship Flight 15 is set for Nov 13 and will try to catch both the booster and the ship, which SpaceX calls the first full recovery of an orbital launch system; it also carries 26 Starlink V3 satellites (Space.com).</li><li>Demand is not only SpaceX's story: CoreWeave beat and raised on Nov 11 with revenue up 168% (your themes doc).</li></ul>\n<p><strong>What would make it worse</strong></p>\n<ul><li>The run-rate. Q4 capex of $35–40B is more than twice Q3's entire revenue. My rough arithmetic: $89.9B covers a little over two quarters at that pace before new money is needed.</li><li>More borrowing. TheStreet reported a commitment to roughly $40B of GPU financing with Apollo for chips due in the first half of 2027, and warned the chips could arrive before the data centers are ready.</li><li>Texas. Governor-elect Gina Hinojosa takes office Jan 19 and has promised a moratorium on all data center approvals until the Legislature acts (Texas Tribune). TheStreet reported a Goldman Sachs estimate that state and local moratoria could touch about 45% of planned national capacity.</li><li>Rates. The Fed is expected to raise again in December; Chair Warsh has said rates do bite on AI credit, and Bowman flagged more than $800B of AI debt and circular financing.</li><li>Two customers are over half of revenue. If either slows, the payback sums change.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>How this company is placed against it</strong></p>\n<ul><li>It has more cash than anyone else building at this pace, but it is also burning it faster: capex was $48.1B in nine months against $13.8B a year earlier.</li><li>It bought Cursor for $42.5B and pays no dividend and bought back no stock, so every dollar is going into the build.</li><li>Its controlling holder can raise money or merge without asking you. That makes funding easier to arrange and gives outside holders no say in the terms.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"money-plans\">Money plans</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Capital spending:</strong> $19.6B in Q3; $48.1B in nine months; $35–40B guided for Q4. No 2027 figure.</li><li><strong>Debt:</strong> $38.0B, up from $22.0B. Cash and securities about $89.9B.</li><li><strong>Buybacks and dividend:</strong> None.</li><li><strong>Deals:</strong> Cursor bought for $42.5B. A Tesla combination is being studied by special committees at both companies.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"people-and-governance\">People and governance</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>CEO:</strong> Elon Musk, founder. He is CEO, chief technical officer and chairman of the board at once (RTÉ, citing the IPO filing). Public company only since June 2026.</li><li><strong>CFO:</strong> Bret Johnsen. Gwynne Shotwell is the other senior executive that pension funds addressed alongside Musk and the CFO; her title was not in the pages read.</li><li><strong>Successor named:</strong> None disclosed. Per a governance write-up of the prospectus (SoftwareSeni), the super-voting shares do not convert on death or transfer to heirs, and the prospectus does not say who would hold them.</li><li><strong>Control:</strong> Dual-class. Class B shares carry 10 votes each, Class A one. The same write-up puts Musk at roughly 42% of the equity and about 79% of the votes, holding 93.6% of the Class B. The 10-Q confirms a controlling shareholder and the 10 votes; his exact voting share was not found in a primary source. SpaceX uses the controlled-company exemption, so it does not need a majority-independent board. It is incorporated in Texas, the charter requires arbitration for shareholder disputes, and there is no sunset on the structure. Removing Musk as CEO or chairman takes a majority of Class B votes, which he holds.</li><li><strong>Objections on record:</strong> On May 13, 2026 the heads of CalPERS and the New York City and New York State pension systems wrote to the company objecting to the structure as extreme and asking for one share, one vote. The structure was not changed.</li><li><strong>Recent changes or investigations:</strong> No executive departure found. Special committees on a Tesla combination (Musk has said a deal is not a certainty). The 10-Q lists the lawsuits and the EU fine noted above. TheStreet reported that Musk spent $446 million backing candidates in the November midterms and that key allies lost in Texas; that is a political exposure tied to one person.</li><li><strong>How much rests on one person:</strong> Almost everything. This is the clearest key-person and control risk of any company you track: one founder holds the three top roles and the votes, his other companies can transact with this one, and there is no named successor. You asked after Nvidia whether leadership risk could be seen in advance. Here it is written into the charter.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"does-the-chart-agree\">Does the chart agree?</h3>\n<figure class=\"chart\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" data-ticker=\"SPCX\" viewBox=\"0 0 760 400\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"SPCX · daily since listing · my lines chart\" font-family=\"Arial, sans-serif\"><text x=\"8\" y=\"18\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#20242C\" class=\"c-ink\">SPCX · daily since listing · my lines</text><line x1=\"8\" x2=\"610\" y1=\"374.0\" y2=\"374.0\" stroke=\"#E3DACB\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-grid\"/><line x1=\"8\" x2=\"610\" y1=\"289.0\" y2=\"289.0\" stroke=\"#E3DACB\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-grid\"/><line x1=\"8\" x2=\"610\" y1=\"204.0\" y2=\"204.0\" stroke=\"#E3DACB\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-grid\"/><line x1=\"8\" x2=\"610\" y1=\"119.0\" y2=\"119.0\" stroke=\"#E3DACB\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-grid\"/><line x1=\"8\" x2=\"610\" y1=\"34.0\" y2=\"34.0\" stroke=\"#E3DACB\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-grid\"/><line x1=\"10.4\" x2=\"10.4\" y1=\"174.6\" y2=\"245.4\" stroke=\"#2F6E4A\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-up\"/><rect x=\"8.9\" y=\"215.2\" width=\"3.0\" height=\"28.5\" fill=\"#2F6E4A\" class=\"c-up\"/><line x1=\"15.2\" x2=\"15.2\" y1=\"131.6\" y2=\"195.9\" stroke=\"#2F6E4A\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-up\"/><rect x=\"13.7\" y=\"133.0\" width=\"3.0\" height=\"54.1\" fill=\"#2F6E4A\" 